Overview:
  An Integration Module encapsulates all communication with one external
  system (bank, MFO, CaptainUp, Mailgun, Slack). It owns request building,
  response parsing, error handling, retry, audit logging, and configuration.
  The module exposes a clean API to the rest of the system and hides all
  external API details behind explicit boundaries.

Rules:

  [IM1] An integration module MUST have an explicit boundary — no external
        system details leak into other modules.

  [IM2] All endpoints, auth, and timeouts MUST be configurable via settings,
        not hardcoded.

  [IM3] Request building MUST use field mapping (internal_field ->
        external_field) with transformation rules, not manual payload assembly.

  [IM4] Response processing MUST map external fields back to internal fields
        using the same mapping engine.

  [IM5] Every external call MUST be auditable: request_id, request_data,
        response_body, execution_time_ms, is_successful.

  [IM6] Retry MUST be bounded: max_attempts, exponential backoff, and dead-
        letter queue after exhaustion.

  [IM7] Pre-check and status polling MUST be config-driven (settings JSON),
        not code branches.

  [IM8] Webhook processing MUST validate signatures (HMAC) before processing.

  [IM9] Daily limits MUST be enforced at the integration level, not globally.

  [IM10] The module MUST be independently testable with mocked HTTP clients.

Architecture:

  IntegrationModule:
    owns:
      - HTTP client configuration (base_url, timeout, auth, content_type)
      - Request field mapping and transformation
      - Response field mapping and transformation
      - Retry policy (max_attempts, backoff, dead-letter)
      - Audit log (request/response/execution_time)
      - Pre-check execution (optional, config-driven)
      - Status polling (optional, config-driven)
      - Webhook processing (optional, signature validation)
      - Daily limit enforcement

    api:
      - execute(integration, payload) -> {success, data, error}
      - executePreCheck(integration, values) -> {passed, message}
      - checkStatus(integration, externalId) -> {status, code}
      - processWebhook(integrationName, data) -> {mapped_fields}

    deps:
      - http-client (BaseHttpService / Guzzle)
      - field-mapping (IntegrationField model)
      - audit-log (IntegrationLog model)
      - settings (Integration settings JSON)

Components:

  IntegrationConfig:
    - alias (unique identifier)
    - api_url, http_method, content_type
    - auth_type, auth_value, api_key_header
    - timeout, retry_attempts, retry_delay
    - webhook_url, webhook_secret
    - settings JSON (pre_check, status_check, response mapping)

  IntegrationField:
    - field_type (request | response)
    - internal_field, external_field, field_path
    - data_type, is_required, default_value
    - transformation_rule (uppercase, digits_only, remove_plus, etc.)
    - sort_order

  IntegrationLog:
    - request_id (correlation ID)
    - integration_id, quiz_params_id, quiz_redirect_id
    - request_type (EXECUTE, PRE_CHECK, STATUS_CHECK, WEBHOOK, MAIL)
    - request_url, request_method, request_headers, request_body
    - response_status, response_headers, response_body
    - execution_time_ms, is_successful, error_message
    - retry_count, created_at

  FieldMappingEngine:
    - resolve(requestFields, params, redirect, requestId) -> payload
    - mapResponse(responseFields, body) -> internalFields
    - transform(value, rule) -> transformedValue
    - setNestedValue(data, path, value) -> data

Workflow:

  execute:
    1. Validate integration is active
    2. Check daily limit
    3. Check user daily limit (if applicable)
    4. Generate request_id
    5. Build request payload via field mapping
    6. Create audit log
    7. Execute HTTP request with retry
    8. Map response fields
    9. Update audit log with response
    10. Return result

  executePreCheck:
    1. Validate pre_check config exists and enabled
    2. Resolve URL (relative -> absolute via base_urls)
    3. Build request from check_fields configuration
    4. Execute HTTP request
    5. Evaluate result against success_field / success_value
    6. Log result to integration_log
    7. Return {passed, message, raw_response}

  checkStatus:
    1. Check cache for existing response
    2. Resolve external ID (request_id, FIN, phone, loan_request_id)
    3. Build status check request from config
    4. Execute HTTP request
    5. Map status/code using status_mapping / code_mapping
    6. Update redirect status if configured
    7. Log result to integration_log
    8. Return {status, status_text, code, code_text}

  processWebhook:
    1. Validate webhook signature (HMAC-SHA256)
    2. Create audit log
    3. Map response fields using active response field mappings
    4. Return mapped internal fields

Best Practices:

  - Use circuit breaker for persistent external API failures
  - Implement idempotency keys for all write operations
  - Cache integration config in Redis for hot-path performance
  - Use database transactions for audit log creation
  - Separate read/write models (CQRS) for high-throughput integrations
  - Version integration configs to support gradual rollout
  - Use feature flags for new integration activation

Anti-Patterns:

  - God integration class that handles all operations
  - Hardcoded URLs, auth, or field mappings
  - Synchronous-only processing (no async fallback)
  - Missing audit trail
  - Retry without backoff or max attempts
  - Direct database access from integration handlers
  - Coupling integration logic to UI/controller layer

State: +
